editorial:
## Three pointers, four lines, and the order is the problem

```cpp
ListNode* reverse(ListNode* head) {
    ListNode* prev = nullptr;
    while (head) {
        ListNode* next = head->next;   // 1. SAVE, or you lose the rest
        head->next = prev;             // 2. flip this link
        prev = head;                   // 3. prev advances
        head = next;                   // 4. head advances
    }
    return prev;                       // prev is the new head
}
```

Step 1 is the one people omit. Without saving `next` first, assigning `head->next = prev` destroys the only reference to the remainder of the list — everything after the current node becomes unreachable.

## Printing without a trailing space

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode* next = nullptr;
    explicit ListNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    ListNode* head = nullptr;
    ListNode* tail = nullptr;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int v;
        cin >> v;
        ListNode* node = new ListNode(v);
        if (!head) head = tail = node;
        else { tail->next = node; tail = node; }
    }

    ListNode* prev = nullptr;
    while (head) {
        ListNode* next = head->next;
        head->next = prev;
        prev = head;
        head = next;
    }

    bool first = true;
    for (ListNode* p = prev; p; p = p->next) {
        if (!first) cout << ' ';
        cout << p->val;
        first = false;
    }
    cout << '\n';
}
```

That `first` flag emits separators *between* values rather than after each one. An empty list prints just the newline, which is the expected output for `n = 0`.

## The recursive version, and why not to use it

```cpp
ListNode* reverse_rec(ListNode* head) {
    if (!head || !head->next) return head;
    ListNode* rest = reverse_rec(head->next);
    head->next->next = head;
    head->next = nullptr;
    return rest;
}
```

Elegant, and O(n) **stack** depth. At `n = 5000` it is fine; at 10^5 it overflows. The iterative version is O(1) space and has no such limit, so it is the default.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Iterative | O(n) | O(1) |
| Recursive | O(n) | O(n) stack |

## Pitfalls

- **Not saving `next` before flipping.**
- **Returning `head` instead of `prev`.** At loop exit `head` is null; `prev` is the new head.
- **A trailing space** on the output line. Harmless here because the judge normalises trailing whitespace, but a bad habit.

