editorial:
## The dummy-head trick

The awkward part of building a list is that the first node is special — there is no previous node to attach it to. A **dummy head** removes that case entirely: attach to `dummy.next` like any other link, and return `dummy.next` at the end.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode* next = nullptr;
    explicit ListNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

ListNode* read_list() {
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    ListNode* head = nullptr;
    ListNode* tail = nullptr;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int v;
        cin >> v;
        ListNode* node = new ListNode(v);
        if (!head) head = tail = node;
        else { tail->next = node; tail = node; }
    }
    return head;
}

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    ListNode* a = read_list();
    ListNode* b = read_list();

    // Stack-allocated dummy: no `new`, no leak, and `dummy.next` is the
    // real head once we are done.
    ListNode dummy(0);
    ListNode* tail = &dummy;

    while (a && b) {
        // `<=` rather than `<` keeps the merge STABLE — equal values keep
        // list a's element first. Not observable here, but it is the habit
        // that makes merge sort stable.
        if (a->val <= b->val) { tail->next = a; a = a->next; }
        else { tail->next = b; b = b->next; }
        tail = tail->next;
    }

    // At most one list is non-empty; attach the whole remainder at once
    // rather than looping. The other is null, which is the correct terminator.
    tail->next = a ? a : b;

    bool first = true;
    for (ListNode* p = dummy.next; p; p = p->next) {
        if (!first) cout << ' ';
        cout << p->val;
        first = false;
    }
    cout << '\n';
}
```

## Why `tail->next = a ? a : b` is enough

The `while` exits as soon as either list is exhausted. The remaining list is already sorted and every one of its values is at least as large as everything merged so far — so it can be appended wholesale in O(1). Looping through it would be correct but pointless.

If both are exhausted, both are null, and `tail->next` becomes null, correctly terminating the list.

## Edge cases

- Both empty → prints an empty line. The dummy makes `dummy.next` null with no special case.
- One empty → the `while` never runs and the whole non-empty list is attached directly.

## Complexity

O(n + m) time — each node is visited once. O(1) extra space: the nodes are relinked, not copied.

## Why not copy into a vector and sort

It works, and it is O((n+m) log(n+m)) instead of O(n+m), discarding the fact that the inputs are already sorted. The merge *is* the linear-time step at the heart of merge sort; recognising it is the point of the problem.

