## Floyd's tortoise and hare

Move one pointer one step at a time and another two steps at a time. If there is a cycle, the fast pointer eventually laps the slow one and they land on the same node. If there is no cycle, the fast pointer runs off the end.

```cpp
bool has_cycle(ListNode* head) {
    ListNode* slow = head;
    ListNode* fast = head;

    // Checking BOTH fast and fast->next is what makes this safe: the body
    // dereferences fast->next->next, and on an even-length list fast->next
    // is the last node, whose next is null.
    while (fast && fast->next) {
        slow = slow->next;
        fast = fast->next->next;
        if (slow == fast) return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

## Why they must meet

This is the part worth understanding rather than memorising. Once both pointers are inside the cycle, consider the gap between them measured forward along the cycle. Each step, `slow` advances 1 and `fast` advances 2, so the gap **shrinks by exactly 1** per step. A quantity that decreases by 1 each step and lives in a finite range must reach 0 — and gap 0 means they are on the same node.

That also shows why 2 is not special: any speed greater than 1 works, as long as the gap strictly decreases. Speed 2 is just the simplest.

## Comparing nodes, not values

`slow == fast` compares **pointers**. Comparing `slow->val == fast->val` would report a cycle for any list with a duplicate value — a wrong answer on `1 1`, which has no cycle.

## The hash-set alternative

```cpp
// O(n) time but O(n) space — and it needs a container keyed on pointers.
unordered_set<ListNode*> seen;
for (ListNode* p = head; p; p = p->next) {
    if (!seen.insert(p).second) return true;
}
return false;
```

Correct and easier to reason about. Floyd's wins on space: O(1) versus O(n), which is the whole reason the technique exists.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Floyd's | O(n) | **O(1)** |
| Hash set | O(n) | O(n) |

## Finding where the cycle starts

The follow-up (LeetCode 142) uses the same machinery: after they meet, reset one pointer to the head and advance both one step at a time. They meet at the cycle's entry point. The proof is a short bit of algebra on the distances, and it is worth doing once.

## Pitfalls

- **Not checking `fast->next`** before `fast->next->next`. Dereferences null on even-length lists.
- **Comparing values instead of pointers.**
- **Starting `fast` at `head->next`** — a common variant that then needs a different loop condition. Starting both at `head` is simpler and handles `n = 0` and `n = 1` with no special case.

