## The gap technique

A singly linked list cannot be walked backwards, so "k-th from the end" is not directly addressable. The trick: advance one pointer `k` steps ahead, then move both together. When the leading pointer reaches the end, the trailing one is exactly `k` nodes behind it.

To *delete* a node you need the node **before** it, so put the trailing pointer on a dummy head. That also handles the case where the node to remove is the head itself — which is otherwise a separate branch.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode* next = nullptr;
    explicit ListNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    ListNode* head = nullptr;
    ListNode* tail = nullptr;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int v;
        cin >> v;
        ListNode* node = new ListNode(v);
        if (!head) head = tail = node;
        else { tail->next = node; tail = node; }
    }
    int k;
    cin >> k;

    // The dummy is what makes "remove the head" need no special case.
    ListNode dummy(0);
    dummy.next = head;

    ListNode* lead = &dummy;
    ListNode* trail = &dummy;

    // Open a gap of exactly k links.
    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) lead = lead->next;

    // Advance together until lead is on the LAST node. trail then sits just
    // before the k-th from the end.
    while (lead->next) {
        lead = lead->next;
        trail = trail->next;
    }

    trail->next = trail->next->next;      // unlink

    bool first = true;
    for (ListNode* p = dummy.next; p; p = p->next) {
        if (!first) cout << ' ';
        cout << p->val;
        first = false;
    }
    cout << '\n';
}
```

## Why the loop stops at `lead->next`

Stopping when `lead` itself becomes null would leave `trail` one position too far forward, deleting the wrong node. Stopping while `lead` is still on the last node leaves `trail` on the predecessor of the target — which is exactly what the unlink needs.

Worth dry-running on `n = 1, k = 1`: `lead` moves one step to the only node, `lead->next` is null so the loop never runs, `trail` is still the dummy, and `dummy.next = nullptr`. The list becomes empty. Correct, with no branch for it.

## The two-pass version

Count the nodes, then walk to position `n - k`. Simpler to reason about, and perfectly acceptable:

```cpp
int length = 0;
for (ListNode* p = head; p; p = p->next) ++length;
ListNode dummy(0);
dummy.next = head;
ListNode* before = &dummy;
for (int i = 0; i < length - k; ++i) before = before->next;
before->next = before->next->next;
```

The one-pass version matters when the list is a stream you cannot re-read. Otherwise either is fine — and the two-pass one is harder to get wrong.

## Complexity

O(n) time, O(1) space, one traversal.

## Pitfalls

- **No dummy head**, forcing a special case when `k == n`.
- **Advancing the gap by `k + 1` or `k - 1`.** Off by one; dry-run `n = 2, k = 1` and `n = 2, k = 2`.
- **Stopping the second loop on `lead` rather than `lead->next`.**
- **Not freeing the removed node.** Irrelevant to the judge, and the right habit everywhere else.

