editorial:
## Three techniques, composed

This problem is the reason the linked-list lesson teaches find-the-middle and reversal separately: the answer is those two plus a merge.

1. **Find the middle** with fast and slow pointers.
2. **Reverse the second half.**
3. **Merge the two halves alternately.**

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode* next = nullptr;
    explicit ListNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    ListNode* head = nullptr;
    ListNode* tail = nullptr;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int v;
        cin >> v;
        ListNode* node = new ListNode(v);
        if (!head) head = tail = node;
        else { tail->next = node; tail = node; }
    }

    if (head && head->next) {
        // --- 1. find the middle ---------------------------------------
        // slow ends on the last node of the FIRST half, which is what lets
        // us cut cleanly below. Starting fast at head->next rather than head
        // is what biases it that way on even lengths.
        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head->next;
        while (fast && fast->next) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;
        }

        ListNode* second = slow->next;
        slow->next = nullptr;              // cut: first half now terminates

        // --- 2. reverse the second half --------------------------------
        ListNode* prev = nullptr;
        while (second) {
            ListNode* next = second->next;
            second->next = prev;
            prev = second;
            second = next;
        }
        second = prev;

        // --- 3. merge alternately --------------------------------------
        // The first half is never shorter than the reversed second half, so
        // driving the loop on `second` is safe.
        ListNode* first = head;
        while (second) {
            ListNode* f_next = first->next;
            ListNode* s_next = second->next;
            first->next = second;
            second->next = f_next;
            first = f_next;
            second = s_next;
        }
    }

    bool first_out = true;
    for (ListNode* p = head; p; p = p->next) {
        if (!first_out) cout << ' ';
        cout << p->val;
        first_out = false;
    }
    cout << '\n';
}
```

## The cut is the subtle part

`slow->next = nullptr` is mandatory. Without it the first half still runs into the second, the merge walks into nodes it has already relinked, and you get a cycle — which shows up as a hang or a flood of output, not a wrong answer.

## Why `fast = head->next`

On an even-length list `1 2 3 4`, starting `fast` at `head` leaves `slow` on node 3, splitting as `1 2 3` / `4`. Starting at `head->next` leaves `slow` on node 2, splitting as `1 2` / `3 4` — the balanced split the merge wants. Dry-run both on four nodes to see it.

## Dry run on `1 2 3 4 5`

- Middle: `slow` ends on node 3 (`1 2 3`), second half `4 5`.
- Reversed second half: `5 4`.
- Merge: `1`, `5`, `2`, `4`, `3`. The first half had one node spare, which lands last.

## Complexity

O(n) time, O(1) space. Copying into a `vector` and re-threading by index is O(n) space and much easier — a legitimate trade when the list is small, and the point of the problem is that it is unnecessary.

## Pitfalls

- **Forgetting the cut**, creating a cycle.
- **Driving the merge loop on `first`** instead of `second`, which overruns on odd lengths.
- **Not guarding `n <= 1`.** A single node is already reordered.

