editorial:
## The naive approach and why it is too slow

Merging list 1 into list 2, then the result into list 3, and so on, re-walks the growing accumulator every time: O(k · N) where `N` is the total node count. With `k = 10^4` that is 10^8 pointer hops on a list that keeps getting longer.

## Approach A: a min-heap of list heads — O(N log k)

At any moment the next value to emit is the smallest among the `k` current heads. A heap gives that in O(log k).

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode* next = nullptr;
    explicit ListNode(int v) : val(v) {}
};

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int k;
    cin >> k;
    // Min-heap of {value, node}. Pairs compare on .first, which is exactly
    // the ordering we want; the pointer is only carried along.
    priority_queue<pair<int, ListNode*>, vector<pair<int, ListNode*>>, greater<>> pq;

    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {
        int n;
        cin >> n;
        ListNode* head = nullptr;
        ListNode* tail = nullptr;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
            int v;
            cin >> v;
            ListNode* node = new ListNode(v);
            if (!head) head = tail = node;
            else { tail->next = node; tail = node; }
        }
        // Only non-empty lists go in. Pushing a null head would crash on pop.
        if (head) pq.push({head->val, head});
    }

    ListNode dummy(0);
    ListNode* tail = &dummy;

    while (!pq.empty()) {
        auto [value, node] = pq.top();
        pq.pop();
        tail->next = node;
        tail = node;
        // Replace it with its successor, keeping the heap at k entries.
        if (node->next) pq.push({node->next->val, node->next});
    }
    tail->next = nullptr;   // the last node still points into its old list

    bool first = true;
    for (ListNode* p = dummy.next; p; p = p->next) {
        if (!first) cout << ' ';
        cout << p->val;
        first = false;
    }
    cout << '\n';
}
```

The `tail->next = nullptr` at the end is easy to miss: the final node was relinked from its original list and may still carry a stale `next`, which would print extra values.

## Approach B: divide and conquer — O(N log k), no heap

Merge the lists in pairs, halving the count each round. Log k rounds, each touching every node once.

```cpp
ListNode* merge_two(ListNode* a, ListNode* b) {
    ListNode dummy(0);
    ListNode* tail = &dummy;
    while (a && b) {
        if (a->val <= b->val) { tail->next = a; a = a->next; }
        else { tail->next = b; b = b->next; }
        tail = tail->next;
    }
    tail->next = a ? a : b;
    return dummy.next;
}

ListNode* merge_all(vector<ListNode*>& lists) {
    if (lists.empty()) return nullptr;
    // Pairwise sweeps: k -> k/2 -> k/4 -> ... -> 1
    while (lists.size() > 1) {
        vector<ListNode*> next_round;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < lists.size(); i += 2) {
            ListNode* second = (i + 1 < lists.size()) ? lists[i + 1] : nullptr;
            next_round.push_back(merge_two(lists[i], second));
        }
        lists = std::move(next_round);
    }
    return lists[0];
}
```

The `dummy` is stack-allocated inside `merge_two`, so returning `dummy.next` is safe — the returned pointer is to a real list node, not to the dummy.

## Why both are O(N log k)

For the heap: every node is pushed and popped once, each costing O(log k) since the heap never exceeds `k` entries.

For divide and conquer: there are log k rounds, and each round's merges together touch all `N` nodes.

The divide-and-conquer version usually wins on constant factor — no heap allocation, better locality. The heap version is the one to reach for when the lists are streams rather than fully materialised.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Sequential merging | O(k · N) | O(1) |
| Min-heap | O(N log k) | O(k) |
| Divide and conquer | O(N log k) | O(k) for the vector |

## Pitfalls

- **Pushing null heads into the heap.** Crashes on the first pop.
- **Forgetting `tail->next = nullptr`** at the end of the heap version.
- **A max-heap.** `greater<>` is what makes it a min-heap.
- **`k = 0`,** or every list empty. Both must print an empty line.

