## Transpose, then reverse each row

The trick that makes this easy: a 90-degree clockwise rotation is exactly a **transpose** followed by a **horizontal flip** of every row. Both are simple in-place operations, and composing them avoids all the index gymnastics of a direct rotation.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<vector<long long>> m(n, vector<long long>(n));
    for (auto& row : m) for (long long& x : row) cin >> x;

    // 1. Transpose: swap across the main diagonal.
    // 🛑 The inner loop starts at c = r + 1, not 0. Starting at 0 visits
    // every pair TWICE, swapping it back and leaving the matrix unchanged.
    for (int r = 0; r < n; ++r)
        for (int c = r + 1; c < n; ++c)
            swap(m[r][c], m[c][r]);

    // 2. Reverse each row.
    for (auto& row : m) reverse(row.begin(), row.end());

    for (int r = 0; r < n; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c)
            cout << m[r][c] << " \n"[c == n - 1];
}
```

## Why it works, on the sample

Start:

```
1 2 3        1 4 7        7 4 1
4 5 6   -->  2 5 8   -->  8 5 2
7 8 9        3 6 9        9 6 3
      transpose      reverse rows
```

The transpose turns rows into columns; reversing then puts those columns in the right order. Reading off the result, the first column of the original (`1 4 7`) has become the first row reversed (`7 4 1`) — which is what a clockwise turn does.

## The `c = r + 1` detail

This is the bug people actually write. With `c` from 0, the pair `(r, c)` is swapped when the loop reaches it and swapped *back* when it reaches `(c, r)`. The net effect is nothing, and the output equals the input — which looks like "my rotation did not run" rather than a logic error.

Iterating the upper triangle only, excluding the diagonal (which never moves), touches each pair exactly once.

## The other direction

- **Counter-clockwise:** transpose, then reverse each *column* — or equivalently reverse the row order first, then transpose.
- **180 degrees:** reverse the row order and reverse each row.

## The direct four-way rotation

For completeness, rotating four cells at a time in rings:

```cpp
for (int layer = 0; layer < n / 2; ++layer) {
    int last = n - 1 - layer;
    for (int i = layer; i < last; ++i) {
        long long top = m[layer][i];
        m[layer][i] = m[n - 1 - i][layer];
        m[n - 1 - i][layer] = m[last][n - 1 - i];
        m[last][n - 1 - i] = m[i][last];
        m[i][last] = top;
    }
}
```

One pass instead of two, and far easier to get an index wrong. Prefer transpose-then-reverse unless you have a reason not to.

## Complexity

O(n²) time — unavoidable, every cell moves. O(1) extra space.

## Pitfalls

- **`c` starting at 0** in the transpose.
- **Reversing columns instead of rows**, which gives a counter-clockwise rotation.
- **Doing the two steps in the wrong order.** Reverse-then-transpose is the counter-clockwise rotation, not the clockwise one.

