## Backtracking on a grid

Try every cell as a starting point. From each, walk in the four directions matching the word one character at a time. Mark a cell as in-use before recursing and **unmark it on the way out**, so a different path may reuse it.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

bool search(vector<string>& grid, const string& word, int r, int c, size_t k) {
    if (k == word.size()) return true;                 // whole word matched
    if (r < 0 || r >= (int)grid.size()) return false;
    if (c < 0 || c >= (int)grid[0].size()) return false;
    if (grid[r][c] != word[k]) return false;           // mismatch, or '#'

    // Mark in place rather than keeping a visited array: O(1) extra space,
    // and '#' never matches a letter, so it doubles as the visited flag.
    char saved = grid[r][c];
    grid[r][c] = '#';

    bool found = search(grid, word, r + 1, c, k + 1)
              || search(grid, word, r - 1, c, k + 1)
              || search(grid, word, r, c + 1, k + 1)
              || search(grid, word, r, c - 1, k + 1);

    // 🛑 Restore on EVERY exit path. Returning early after a success without
    // restoring leaves '#' in the grid and corrupts any later search — a bug
    // that only shows up on the second query, never the first.
    grid[r][c] = saved;
    return found;
}

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int rows, cols;
    cin >> rows >> cols;
    vector<string> grid(rows);
    for (string& row : grid) cin >> row;
    string word;
    cin >> word;

    for (int r = 0; r < rows; ++r)
        for (int c = 0; c < cols; ++c)
            if (search(grid, word, r, c, 0)) {
                cout << "true\n";
                return 0;
            }
    cout << "false\n";
}
```

## Why the bounds checks come first

They are tested *before* `grid[r][c]` is read, and `||` short-circuits, so no out-of-range access can happen. Putting the character comparison first would index the grid with coordinates that may be off the edge.

Doing the checks at the top of the callee — rather than before each recursive call — means four call sites get them for free, which is less code and fewer chances to omit one.

## The single restore is deliberate

Notice there is exactly one `grid[r][c] = saved;`, placed after the four recursive calls and before the only `return`. An implementation that returns `true` early from inside the chain would need a restore on that path too — easy to forget. Structuring it as "compute `found`, restore, return" makes the restore unmissable.

## A useful pruning check

Before searching at all, count the letters: if the grid does not contain enough copies of some letter in the word, the answer is `false` immediately. Cheap, and it turns pathological inputs like a 6×6 grid of `A` with word `AAAAAAAAAAAAAAB` from exponential into instant.

## Complexity

O(rows · cols · 4^L) in the worst case, where `L` is the word length — each cell starts a search that branches up to 4 ways (3 after the first step, since you never go back) per character. The constraints are small precisely because this is exponential.

O(L) space for the recursion stack.

## The follow-up

"Word Search II" asks for many words at once. Running this once per word is too slow; the answer is a **trie** of all the words, walked alongside a single DFS of the grid, so one traversal tests every word and prunes as soon as no word has the current prefix.

## Pitfalls

- **Forgetting to restore the cell.**
- **Checking the character before the bounds.**
- **A separate `visited` array that is never reset** between starting cells.
- **Allowing diagonal moves.** Four directions, not eight.
